.TH GGE\-SPRITE 7 "generalGameEngine" "Engine Manual" "Engine Manual"
.SH NAME
gge\-sprite \- sprite sheets, frames, animation playback, flipping and tinting
.SH DESCRIPTION
A \fBSprite\fR (sprite.h) is a texture sliced into a grid of equal frames; \fBsprite_draw\fR and friends draw one frame at a position. \fBAnim\fR describes a run of frames and its timing; \fBAnimState\fR is the playback position of one animated thing. The module is deliberately small: it indexes and draws, and leaves "which animation should play" to your entity code.
.PP
.SH SHEETS AND FRAMES
Lay out a sheet as rows of equal cells. Frames are numbered left to right, top to bottom, from 0:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
+\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-+      16x16 cells, 64 px wide \-> columns = 4
|  0 |  1 |  2 |  3 |      frame 5 = column 1, row 1
+\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-+      frame = column + row * columns
|  4 |  5 |  6 |  7 |
+\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-+
.EE
.RE
.PP
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
static Sprite hero;
hero = sprite_from(assets_texture("sprites/hero.png"), 16, 16);   /* in on_enter */
sprite_draw(&hero, 5, x, y, 0, FLIP_NONE);                         /* in render */
.EE
.RE
.PP
sprite_from(3) computes \fBcolumns\fR from the texture width. A single image is a one\-frame sheet: \fBsprite_from(tex, w, h)\fR and always frame 0. A Sprite is a small value type: keep one static per sheet per scene, copy it freely, never free it (the texture belongs to the asset cache).
.PP
Face one way in the art and mirror with FLIP_H; that halves the frames a character needs. Rotation (degrees, clockwise, about the frame center) is for projectiles, wheels and pointers; pixel art rotated by non\-right angles looks rough, so prefer drawn frames for characters.
.PP
.SH ANIMATIONS
Define animations as data, one table per sheet, indexed by an enum your entity stores:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
enum { HERO_IDLE, HERO_RUN, HERO_JUMP, HERO_HURT, HERO_ANIM_COUNT };
static const Anim hero_anims[HERO_ANIM_COUNT] = {
  [HERO_IDLE] = { .first = 0,  .count = 4, .frame_time = 0.25f, .loop = true  },
  [HERO_RUN]  = { .first = 4,  .count = 6, .frame_time = 0.08f, .loop = true  },
  [HERO_JUMP] = { .first = 10, .count = 1, .frame_time = 1,     .loop = false },
  [HERO_HURT] = { .first = 11, .count = 2, .frame_time = 0.1f,  .loop = false },
};
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fBfirst\fR is the sheet frame where the run starts, \fBcount\fR how many frames follow it, \fBframe_time\fR seconds per frame, \fBloop\fR whether it repeats or stops on the last frame. A count of 1 is a still.
.PP
The entity carries an \fBAnimState\fR (pointer\-free, so it lives in GameState and saves):
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
/* in the entity's tick, decide; in update, step */
if (!p\->on_ground)             anim_set(&p\->anim, HERO_JUMP);
else if (fabsf(p\->vel.x) > 1)  anim_set(&p\->anim, HERO_RUN);
else                           anim_set(&p\->anim, HERO_IDLE);
anim_step(&p\->anim, &hero_anims[p\->anim.anim], dt);
\&
/* in render */
int frame = anim_frame(&p\->anim, &hero_anims[p\->anim.anim]);
sprite_draw(&hero, frame, sx, sy, 0, p\->facing < 0 ? FLIP_H : FLIP_NONE);
.EE
.RE
.PP
anim_set(3) is a no\-op when that animation is already playing, so calling it every tick from the decision code is the intended use: the animation restarts only when the decision changes. anim_step(3) advances by dt and wraps or stops; a stopped non\-looping animation sets \fBdone\fR, which is how "play HURT, then go back to IDLE" is expressed:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
if (p\->anim.anim == HERO_HURT && !p\->anim.done) ;            /* keep playing */
else ... normal decision ...
.EE
.RE
.PP
Frame timing is in seconds, so it is independent of the tick rate; 0.08 to 0.12 s per frame reads as a run cycle, 0.2 to 0.3 as idle breathing.
.PP
.SH TINTING AND FADING
sprite_draw_tinted(3) multiplies the frame's colors by a Color and its alpha by the tint's alpha, then restores the texture so other draws are unaffected:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sprite_draw_tinted(&hero, frame, x, y, rgba(255, 255, 255, 128), flip);   /* half transparent */
sprite_draw_tinted(&hero, frame, x, y, rgb(255, 60, 60), flip);            /* damage flash */
sprite_draw_tinted(&team, frame, x, y, team_color[t], flip);               /* recolor a white sheet */
.EE
.RE
.PP
Palette\-free recoloring: draw the character's clothes as a white silhouette layer and tint it per team or per player, with the un\-tinted base drawn beneath. Tinting cannot rotate or scale; draw the layers in the same frame order.
.PP
.SH SCALING
sprite_draw_scaled(3) draws a frame at \fBscale\fR times its size, from the same top\-left corner. Its usual argument is the camera zoom (tilemap_draw uses it that way). Non\-integer scales of pixel art look uneven; keep zoom at 1, 2, 3 or 4.
.PP
.SH ANCHORS
\fBsprite_draw\fR positions the \fBtop\-left\fR corner. If an entity's \fBpos\fR is its feet or center, subtract before drawing: \fBsprite_draw(&hero, f, sx \- 8, sy \- 16, 0, flip)\fR for a 16x16 sprite whose pos is bottom\-center. Pick one convention per game (top\-left is simplest with tilemap_move, which wants a top\-left box) and keep hitboxes consistent with it: a hitbox is usually smaller than the frame, inset from the art.
.PP
.SH TILESETS ARE SPRITES
A tileset is a Sprite whose frame index equals the tile id; Tilemap.tileset is one, built by tilemap_load_csv(3) or by you (gge\-tilemap(7)). Keep frame 0 blank in a tileset because tile id 0 means empty.
.PP
.SH ONE SHEET, MANY THINGS
A sheet can hold several kinds of thing; each kind's Anim table just starts at a different \fBfirst\fR. Fewer textures mean fewer state changes when drawing, but it makes no measurable difference at this scale; organize sheets for the artist, not the renderer.
.PP
.SH PITFALLS
.IP \(bu 2
Wrong \fBcolumns\fR: the sheet width is not a multiple of the frame width, so frames drift. Check the image size.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBanim_step\fR called from render: the animation then runs at display rate and does not save. Step in update.
.IP \(bu 2
Two entities sharing one AnimState: each needs its own; the Anim table is shared, the state is not.
.IP \(bu 2
Forgetting \fBdone\fR: a non\-looping animation that finished stays on its last frame until anim_set is called with a different id.
.IP \(bu 2
Flip with rotation: flipping is applied after rotation, about the destination center.
